@H1 6. AEQUSPINE - ONE SYNERGETIC CORRIDOR SYSTEM
@P DIRECTIVE. Waterways, drainage, electricity (smart grid; each home an independent generator feeding excess outward; interoperable and adaptable), internet (same model) and agriculture are designed together with every other surround. RULE OF SYNERGY: no network gets a private corridor if it can share one without breaking a separation rule, and every output of one network is checked as an input of another (6.7).
@H2 6.1 Cross-sections (schematic S-02) and layer stack
{{table:sections}}
@P LAYER STACK, top to bottom: canopy (PV colonnade + luminaire, 4.5 m) | surface (permeable pavers, ribbons, swales) | TROUGH 0.9 m deep (LV power + fibre microducts, 100% spare ducts, lockable lids) | WET STRIP {{d.wet_depth}} m deep (potable, non-potable, sewer under liftable pavers) | storm water runs in the surface swale: no storm pipe.
{{table:separation}}
@H2 6.2 Waterways, drainage and water: the sponge system
@L SLOW, SPREAD, SINK, STORE, CLEAN, REUSE. Street surfaces infiltrate 78-80% of rain; roofs feed tanks; kerbless edges shed the rest to verge swales and bioretention; wedge ponds store the surplus; infiltrated water is banked as managed aquifer recharge (MAR).
@L POTABLE FIRST. Irrigation may never draw MAR below a reserve of 3 months of potable use. Result: potable self-supply {{k.potable_ssr|.0%}}, and a {{res.water.drought.years}}-year drought at {{res.water.drought.factor|.0%}} of normal rain with potable restricted to {{cfg.water.drought_restrict_lpd}} L/p/d leaves {{res.water.drought.unmet_pot_pct|.1f}}% unmet.
@L RECLAIMED LOOP. Greywater ({{cfg.water.grey_lpd}} L/p/d, {{cfg.water.grey_treat_eff|.0%}} treated) serves toilets, laundry and then irrigation; blackwater (toilet + kitchen) goes to the ward digester, effluent is polished in constructed wetlands (reed, bulrush, palmiet) and applied by drip. Solids become compost / biochar / struvite.
@L PONDS + FLOATING PV. Ponds hold {{cfg.water.pond_m3_pp}} m3 per person ({{d.ponds_ha_city|.0f}} ha city-wide); {{cfg.water.pond_pv_cover|.0%}} PV cover cuts open-water evaporation by about {{d.pond_evap_cut|.0f}}% and adds PV yield.
@L WATERWAYS. Natural streams stay as the blue spine: no building in the 1:100 floodplain; >= 32 m buffer each side as a minimum (urban EIA trigger distance; verify local rule); wedge ponds sit on-line as flood storage; crossings clear-span (7.7).
@L PUBLIC WATER. Drinking points every <= 200 m along seams, at every court and node ({{res.facilities.drinking_points|,.0f}} city-wide); hygiene hubs (7.5) discharge to the reclaimed loop.
@L WINTER-RAIN TRUTH. Irrigation is the binding water load ({{res.water.irr_m3_pp|.0f}} m3/p/yr vs potable {{res.water.pot_m3_pp|.0f}}). {{k.irrigation_ssr|.0%}} is self-supplied; the Dec-Mar top-up ({{res.water.ext_m3_pp.irr|.1f}} m3/p/yr) is external until ponds grow or crops shift to the rain-fed winter calendar (L-16).
{{table:water_balance}}
{{table:flood}}
@H2 6.3 Electricity: smart grid with every home a generator feeding outward
@L HOME ENERGY NODE (HEN). Each dwelling: {{d.pv_kwp_dwelling|.1f}} kWp PV on a north-facing mono-pitch roof, {{res.energy.hen.battery_kwh|.1f}} kWh battery, {{res.energy.hen.inverter_kw|.1f}} kW grid-forming hybrid inverter, islanding switch, smart meter, local controller. It can run alone, and exports any excess to the cell.
@L CELL MICROGRID. LV 400/230 V in the trough; {{res.energy.hen.cell_battery_mwh|.1f}} MWh cell battery (N+1 grid-forming, black start); adaptive protection (inverter fault currents are small). Ward ties and one outer-grid interconnect per ward let the city feed excess outward ({{res.energy.export_kwh_pp|,.0f}} kWh/p/yr surplus) and draw only {{res.energy.import_kwh_pp|.0f}} kWh/p/yr.
@L EQUIDISTRIBUTION. Pooled canopy output is allocated per capita, so a flat-dweller and a house-dweller have equal shares; roofs alone cover demand up to 4 storeys (table below).
@L ADAPTABLE + INTEROPERABLE. Only open standards at every interface (table); the controller is open firmware; a DC bus (T2) can be added without re-trenching because ducts are spare.
@L SOLAR PLACEMENT, ranked: (1) roofs: optimum tilt {{res.solar.opt_tilt}} deg, no extra land, {{res.energy.placements.0.kwh_kwp|.0f}} kWh/kWp; (2) seam colonnade over the cycle track: shade + {{res.energy.placements.1.kwh_kwp|.0f}} kWh/kWp; (3) floating PV on ponds: +4% yield, less evaporation; (4) agri-PV on wedge farms: GCR 0.35, clearance 3.5 m, 78% yield, shade-tolerant crops; (5) vertical facades last: {{d.facade_ratio|.0%}} of optimum.
@L SEASON. June PV is {{d.pv_jun_dec|.0%}} of December: storage covers nights and cloud runs; CHP biogas ({{res.energy.chp_kwh_e_pp|.0f}} kWh/p/yr) and outer-grid partnership cover rare long dull spells ({{res.energy.lold_days}} days/yr with any import, longest run {{res.energy.max_import_run_days}} d).
{{table:energy_balance}}
{{table:standards}}
@H2 6.4 Internet and data (same outward-feeding, interoperable model)
@L DIG ONCE. Fibre microducts share the trough with power: 100% spare ducts, one opening, one inspection.
@L EVERY BUILDING A NODE. Fibre to each home (open-access, any provider) plus a community mesh access point per building that relays neighbours, so the network grows from the edges and survives a trunk cut.
@L EDGE HUB PER CELL. Micro data room at the node, powered from the cell microgrid, waste heat to tunnel houses or hygiene-hub hot water; local-first routing keeps services working offline.
@L SENSOR LAYER (LoRaWAN): pond and swale level, flow, soil moisture, air, PV, battery, structure, waste bins. Sensors feed irrigation, flood routing, dimming, maintenance.
@L SECURITY. Zero-trust segmentation, open auditable firmware, signed updates, manual local override for every actuator. Ledger (AequChain) records energy/water accounting; personal data stays local.
@H2 6.5 Agriculture and fauna integrated with every network
@L WATER: reclaimed drip, pond + swale sources, sensor scheduling. NUTRIENTS: digestate, compost, biochar, struvite close the phosphorus loop to {{res.food.nutrient_closure_diet.1|.0%}} of the plant diet. ENERGY: agri-PV, pump timing to the solar peak (water is the cheapest battery), cold chain. HEAT: CHP + data heat to tunnel houses.
@L PUBLIC REALM IS PRODUCTIVE: forest-garden verges, food-forest swales, court gardens, roof gardens, market hall. FAUNA: honeybees, black soldier fly (organics to protein), vermicompost, poultry, permitted freshwater fish, amphibian ponds, dark wildlife corridors.
